Work queue (in this order, per Andres - new items get appended at the end unless he
says otherwise). Reordered 2026-08-02.

Tier 1 - self-contained features/fixes:

- session.php/index.php: check whether the "Show tool usage details" toggle's label
  should actually read "toggle tool outputs" instead - verify against what it really
  does (hides tool_result blocks via the hide-tool-details CSS class; tool_use blocks
  are unaffected) before renaming, since the current name may already be accurate for
  half of what people expect it to do.

Tier 2 - bigger or investigative items:

- index.php: make the whole session-row "card" a link to its transcript page,
  except for known interactive bits (Kill button, show-last-3 toggle, blocked-prompt
  option buttons, etc.) which should keep working normally without navigating away.
- index.php: list all known sessions grouped by project folder (not just currently-
  running ones - scan ~/.claude/projects for past sessions per cwd), and offer to
  restart/reattach a listed-but-not-currently-running one via `claude --resume
  <session-id>` in a fresh tmux pane. Overlaps with the "unify sessions/bare
  processes" and reptyr items below (all under the general theme of "surface every
  session, not just ones currently live and app-spawned"), but this one's
  specifically about PAST/dormant sessions, not currently-running bare ones -
  worth reconciling into one coherent design rather than three separate features
  before building any of them.
- index.php: unify the "sessions" (cc-* tmux, app-tracked) and "Other claude
  processes" (bare) lists into one. The SessionStart hook fires for every claude
  session on the box now, not just ones this app spawned, which makes this
  tractable: generalize the hook to register a sidecar for ANY claude session
  running inside a tmux pane - key it off the pane's own tmux session name (via
  `tmux display-message`), not just the app-set CSM_SESSION_NAME env var, which
  only app-spawned sessions have - then fold those into the main list with full
  features (transcript, compose, answer-prompt, mode toggle all work via tmux
  send-keys/capture-pane regardless of who started the pane). Hard boundary: a
  claude process with NO tmux pane at all (bare terminal/SSH, no wrapper) can
  never get send-keys/capture-pane support - at most a read-only transcript
  link - so a small residual "unmanageable" category likely still remains
  after this.
- Research: use reptyr to close the "no tmux pane at all" gap in the item just
  above, instead of leaving it a permanent residual "unmanageable" category.
  reptyr hijacks a running process's controlling terminal via ptrace and moves
  it to a new pty without killing/restarting it - so a bare claude process
  started in a plain terminal/SSH session (no tmux) could be adopted into a
  fresh tracked tmux pane after the fact, then picked up by the generalized
  SessionStart-hook sidecar registration from the item above like any other
  tmux-paned session. Andres's own starting-point sketch (needs review/
  hardening before use, not vetted yet - e.g. the sudo reptyr requirement,
  ptrace_scope implications, and whether this is safe to expose as a one-click
  dashboard action versus a manual host-side escape hatch):
    #!/bin/bash
    if [ "$1" != "--pid" ] || [ -z "$2" ]; then
        echo "Usage: tmuxify --pid <PID>"
        exit 1
    fi
    PID=$2
    if ! kill -0 "$PID" 2>/dev/null; then
        echo "Error: Process ID $PID does not exist."
        exit 1
    fi
    PROC_NAME=$(ps -p "$PID" -o comm= | tr -d '[:space:]')
    SESSION_NAME="tmuxify_${PROC_NAME}_${PID}"
    echo "Targeting process: $PROC_NAME (PID: $PID)"
    # Stop/background the process, then sever shell ownership so it survives
    # this terminal exiting (TSTP+CONT simulates Ctrl+Z then 'bg').
    kill -TSTP "$PID" 2>/dev/null
    kill -CONT "$PID" 2>/dev/null
    disown "$PID" 2>/dev/null
    tmux new-session -d -s "$SESSION_NAME"
    # sudo is used here because ptrace restrictions usually block non-root hijacking.
    tmux send-keys -t "$SESSION_NAME" "sudo reptyr $PID" C-m
    echo "Success! Process $PID moved to tmux session: $SESSION_NAME"
    sleep 1
    exec tmux attach-session -t "$SESSION_NAME"
- Research: investigate whether subagent calls (Agent/Task tool invocations) are
  retrievable from a session's own JSONL transcript, or only exist in a separate
  sub-transcript file - and more generally, survey what other info sits in these
  JSONL files that this app doesn't currently surface (sidechain/isSidechain
  entries, agent_id/agent_type fields, file-history-snapshot diffs, etc.) that
  might be worth rendering on session.php.
- Research: how worthwhile would a refactor into classes be, or even a full move
  to Laravel 13/Livewire 4, versus the current plain-function/plain-PHP-file
  style this whole app is written in. A real architecture question, not a quick
  investigation; weigh against how much this app is likely to keep growing (it's
  picked up a lot of surface area this session alone) vs. the cost of a
  rewrite/migration for what's currently a personal single-user tool with no
  other contributors.
- Research: how would file uploads work (Andres wants to send an image/file
  from his phone to a session, not just receive them from the transcript).
  Needs a real look at the mechanism - tmux send-keys/paste-buffer only moves
  text, so this isn't just "extend the existing compose box"; worth checking
  whether Claude Code's CLI has any file-drop/paste-image support at the
  terminal level to hook into, or whether this needs an entirely different
  delivery path (e.g. writing the file somewhere on disk and referencing its
  path in a message).
- session.php: after answering a prompt that popped up mid-typing (covering
  the compose textarea while it's focused), the compose textarea sometimes
  comes back too short once the prompt clears - text still there but only a
  sliver of it visible, not the normal auto-grown height. Andres currently
  has to navigate to the dashboard and back to get a normal-sized textarea
  again. Likely related to autoGrowCompose() not re-running (or running
  against a stale/zeroed scrollHeight) at the right moment when the compose
  bar's visibility is toggled back on by renderComposeVisibility().
- Research: now that this app has a PreToolUse hook (see the pending-tool
  full-command-preview feature), survey whether other Claude Code hook events
  (PostToolUse, Notification, UserPromptSubmit, etc) could replace/improve any
  of the pane-scraping this app currently does - e.g. catching a tool result
  right as it lands instead of waiting for the next transcript poll. Stop is
  already ruled out (tried 2026-08-02: Claude Code writes the transcript file
  ~1.7-2s BEFORE the Stop hook fires, the opposite of what you'd assume, so it
  has nothing to add over reading the transcript directly - verify actual
  hook firing order/latency against a real session before building on an
  assumption about it, same as this finding).
- Investigate: Andres reports messages he typed sometimes disappear from
  session.php after navigating to another page and back to the session.
  Needs more detail to reproduce - which messages (his own compose sends,
  or ones typed directly into a real Claude Code session outside the app?),
  how soon after sending, whether a poll cycle happens in between.
- index.php: answering a prompt from the dashboard (both the plain-option
  handler and submitFreetextReply(), lines ~280/324) just replaces the
  prompt UI with a static "Sent - refresh to see the result" note instead
  of actually showing the result - the code's own comment notes there's
  "no live poll on this page yet" for individual session rows. Andres
  wants this fixed; probably means having the dashboard actually poll/
  refresh that session's row after a send instead of leaving a manual-
  refresh note, but worth confirming the exact desired behavior before
  implementing.
- Add a badge to the home-screen icon (via the Badging API,
  navigator.setAppBadge()/clearAppBadge() from src/sw.js's push handler)
  when a push notification has been sent but not yet cleared/seen.
  Caveat worth checking before building: the whole push feature here was
  built specifically for iOS Safari's home-screen-installed PWA mode
  (see the README's cert-trust section), and Badging API support on iOS
  PWAs has historically lagged well behind other platforms - confirm it
  actually works in Andres's real installed-app context before assuming
  this is a straightforward addition on top of the existing sw.js.
- Investigate: Andres reports tapping a push notification on iOS opens
  the dashboard instead of the specific session it was about. sw.js's
  notificationclick handler already looks correct in the code - it reads
  event.notification.data.url (set from the real per-session URL
  check_and_send_pushes() passes to send_push_notification(), e.g.
  /session.php?session=cc-...) and calls clients.openWindow(url)/focus()
  on a matching existing client - so this smells like a known-ish iOS/
  WebKit limitation with openWindow() in home-screen-installed PWA
  (standalone display) mode rather than an app-side bug, but needs
  confirming live on Andres's actual device before assuming that's really
  the cause or looking for a workaround.
